class AppellationDesignation {
  definition {
    A verbal designation of an individual concept (ISO 1087-1:2000,
    3.4.2; ISO 10241-1:2011 §3.4.1.1.6; ISO 704:2022 §7.3.3).

    Per ISO 704:2022 §5.3, individual concepts correspond to a unique
    object (or composition considered unique). Their designations are
    proper names — distinct from terms, which designate general
    concepts.

    Examples (ISO 704:2022 §5.3):
      - "United Nations" (organization)
      - "IBM" (organization)
      - "Statue of Liberty" (monument)
      - "Canada" (geographic region)
      - "Canadian Radio-television and Telecommunications Commission"

    Note: A proper name with a linking element like "and" is still
    ONE individual concept (e.g. "North, Central and South America"
    refers to a single region with three parts). See ISO 704:2022
    §5.3 Example 2.

    Appellations may carry normative status (preferred/admitted/
    deprecated) just like terms (ISO 10241-1:2011 §3.4.1.3 Note 1).

    Distinct from ExpressionDesignation (which designates general
    concepts). Concepts with `concept_type: individual` should use
    AppellationDesignation; concepts with `concept_type: general`
    should use ExpressionDesignation.
  }
  +designation: <<BasicDocument>> LocalizedString [1] {
    definition {
      The proper name text, localized.
    }
  }
  +normativeStatus: NormativeStatus [0..1] {
    definition {
      Preferred, admitted, or deprecated. There can be more than one
      of each (ISO 10241-1:2011 §3.4.1.3).
    }
  }
  +absent: Boolean [0..1] {
    definition {
      Indication that the appellation is missing for this concept in
      this language.
    }
  }
  +fieldofapplication: <<BasicDocument>> LocalizedString [0..1] {
    definition {
      Subject field disambiguator.
    }
  }
  +usageinfo: <<BasicDocument>> LocalizedString [0..1] {
    definition {
      Usage disambiguator.
    }
  }
  +geographicArea: Iso3166Code [0..*] {
    definition {
      Geographic area where the proper name is used.
    }
  }
  +sources: ConceptSource [0..*] {
    definition {
      Bibliographic references.
    }
  }
  +language: Iso639ThreeCharCode [0..1] {
    definition {
      ISO 639-3 language code.
    }
  }
}
